data.content:
## EF Core Cosmos DB Provider: Sync API Removal

Starting with **EF Core 9**, the Azure Cosmos DB provider dropped synchronous I/O support because the underlying Cosmos SDK is async-only. In .NET 10, you could opt in to **sync-over-async** behavior (wrapping async calls synchronously) via:

```csharp
options.UseCosmos(..., cosmosOptions =>
    cosmosOptions.AllowSynchronousIO());
```

**In .NET 11, that opt-in is removed.** All four of your methods will throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime.

### The fix — convert to async:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

### Key changes

| Sync (breaks in .NET 11) | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `ToList()` | `ToListAsync()` |
| `First()` | `FirstAsync()` |
| `Count()` | `CountAsync()` |
| `SaveChanges()` | `SaveChangesAsync()` |

Callers must also be updated to `await` these methods, which means propagating `async/Task` up the call chain (controllers, services, etc.). `Add()` remains synchronous — it only tracks the entity in memory.
